{
  "claimable": true,
  "schema_version": 1,
  "name": "tool_content_hash",
  "description": "Cross-SDK tool_content_hash vectors (obsvr-tool-content-v1) - the sealed evidence of WHICH tool content and arguments a given tool call saw. document_cases pin buildToolContentDocument/computeToolContentHash: expect.canonical is the exact serialized document that gets hashed, expect.hash its SHA-256 (lowercase 64-hex, never truncated), and the two component digests are pinned separately so a failure says WHICH half diverged. equivalence_groups pin the cases that must produce the SAME hash (key order, absent/empty/null arguments, omitted null fields). distinction_cases prove tool_content_hash is NOT the descriptor-pinning hash of tool_pinning.json - different projections, and a behavior-hint change that moves one and not the other. unstable_number_cases pin the numbers that must THROW rather than seal a digest only one language can recompute; a caller on a tool boundary catches the throw and omits the field, because a wrong hash is worse than none - sealed evidence cannot be reissued. Descriptors use MCP wire names (inputSchema), as tool_pinning.json does; harnesses adapt to their own field spelling. Both SDK harnesses (sdk-typescript/tests/unit/tool-content-hash-conformance.test.ts, and the Python twin when it lands) must produce identical results; a divergence is a release blocker unless recorded in conformance/known-divergences.md.",
  "canonical_document": "{\"args_sha256\":<sha256 hex>,\"descriptor_sha256\":<sha256 hex>,\"schema\":\"obsvr-tool-content-v1\",\"tool_name\":<name>}",
  "canonicalization": "RFC 8785-style: recursively sorted keys, no insignificant whitespace, and a cross-SDK-stable number formatter. Deliberately NOT the frozen rules-hash canonicalizer. That one now agrees across languages too, but it agrees by NORMALIZING - an integer past 2^53 is rounded to the float64 JS parsed it as - so two documents differing only above that boundary canonicalize the same. Tool descriptors and call arguments are attacker-shaped JSON, where that collision is a rug-pull the pin cannot see, so this formatter refuses such values instead (see unstable_number_cases).",
